== Canal reconstruction ==

[[File:Balboa_Canal_reconstruction_1952.jpg|thumb|300px|Reconstruction of the Balboa Canal lock system, c. 1952. Breislandic engineers and Black Balboan labourers working on the lock gates, concrete mixing and steel reinforcement visible, a temporary cofferdam holding back the water. Construction cranes and scaffolding along the lock chamber, the damaged control tower in the background. Industrial documentary photograph style.]]

The physical condition of the canal at the establishment of the mandate was catastrophic. The lock systems at both the eastern and western ends had been damaged by military action and by a decade of neglect. The channels had silted in; the towpaths and service roads were impassable; the warehouses and loading facilities at Portnou and at Sant Lázaro had been destroyed or looted; and the administrative infrastructure — the records, the signalling systems, the traffic control apparatus — had been lost.

The first High Commissioner, [[Jürgen Heidemann]], was a Breislandic civil engineer who had been appointed on the basis of his technical expertise rather than his diplomatic experience. Heidemann's tenure was devoted almost entirely to the physical reconstruction of the canal. The work was funded by the [[General War Reparations Fund]], established under the [[Treaty of Klow]], and by additional contributions from the Adonian Community's member states, which had a direct commercial interest in the canal's reopening.

The reconstruction proceeded in three phases. The first phase (1950–1953) focused on the clearance of debris, the repair of the lock systems, and the dredging of the main channel. The work was labour-intensive and was performed by a workforce drawn from the canal zone's civilian population, supplemented by contracted labourers from the Sant Cristòfor mandate. The second phase (1953–1956) involved the reconstruction of the canal's ancillary infrastructure: the warehouses, the loading facilities, the signalling systems, and the administrative buildings. The third phase (1956–1958) addressed the canal's operational capacity: the deepening of the channel to accommodate larger vessels, the installation of modern traffic control systems, and the training of a permanent canal operations staff.

The canal resumed partial operation in 1953, allowing limited commercial traffic to pass through the eastern locks. Full operation was restored in 1956, and the canal's traffic volume increased steadily thereafter. By 1958, the canal was handling approximately 80 per cent of its pre-war traffic volume, and the revenue from canal tolls — which was retained by the mandate administration to fund its operations — provided a degree of fiscal independence that the Sant Cristòfor mandate, dependent on the General War Reparations Fund, did not enjoy.

== Population and economy ==

[[File:Portnou_harbour_1955.jpg|thumb|300px|The reconstructed Portnou harbour, c. 1955. Restored warehouse facilities along the waterfront, merchant vessels at dock, Black Balboan and Sinchewese dockworkers loading cargo, mandate administration buildings flying the Adonian Community flag. The partially rebuilt harbour-front commercial district visible beyond the docks. Documentary photograph style, bright tropical light.]]

The canal zone's civilian population at the establishment of the mandate was approximately 280,000, concentrated in the towns of [[Portnou]] and [[Sant Lázaro]] and in the smaller settlements along the canal corridor. The population was sharply divided along racial lines: the Black Balboan community formed the majority, particularly in the agricultural settlements south of the canal and in the working-class districts of Portnou, while the white Balboan community — the descendants of the Volisanian colonial planter class and the commercial families who had dominated Portnou before the war — retained a significant presence in the town's commercial and professional quarters. The mixed Balboan population was proportionally smaller than in the Sant Cristòfor mandate, as the canal zone had been less affected by the intermarriage and social integration that characterised the city's cosmopolitan culture. The Sinchewese community was small, concentrated in the harbour workforce at Portnou, and the Zong population was negligible: the Zong commercial and administrative personnel who had operated the canal under the Zong Balboa government had almost entirely departed during or after the war.

The canal zone's economy was dominated by the canal itself. Portnou, which had been the canal's western terminus and the principal commercial port since 1885, was the zone's largest urban centre. [[Sant Lázaro]], at the canal's eastern entrance, was the zone's second town and served as the eastern gateway to the waterway. Both towns' economies had been devastated by the war: the harbour facilities had been destroyed, the warehouses had been burned, and the commercial population had fled or been displaced. The mandate's reconstruction of the harbour and the canal facilities provided the basis for Portnou's economic recovery, and the town's population grew from approximately 90,000 in 1950 to 150,000 by 1960 as workers returned to the canal operations and the associated service industries. Sant Lázaro, which had been less heavily damaged than Portnou, recovered more quickly and served as the mandate's primary civilian administrative centre during the early reconstruction period.

The agricultural sector south of the canal, which had been managed by Zong-owned plantations during the Zong Balboa period, underwent a transformation similar to that in the Sant Cristòfor mandate: the Zong owners departed, and the land was acquired by the Black Balboan workers and supervisors who had operated it. The mandate administration, which was primarily concerned with the canal, did not intervene in the agricultural transition, and the new Black Balboan landowners operated their farms independently of the mandate's economic planning.

The civilian population's relationship with the mandate administration was strained. The mandate's prioritisation of canal operations over civilian services meant that Portnou and the canal corridor settlements received less investment in housing, education, and healthcare than the Sant Cristòfor mandate territory to the north. The absence of civilian representation in the mandate's governance compounded the grievance: the population of the canal zone had no advisory body, no elected officials, and no formal mechanism for petitioning the High Commissioner. The contrast with the Sant Cristòfor mandate, which had a Consultative Assembly and a more responsive administration, was a source of resentment.

== The International City of Areza ==

[[File:Areza_construction_1965.jpg|thumb|300px|Construction of the Adonian Community Assembly building in Areza, c. 1965. Modernist concrete and glass structure rising on the north shore of Lake Alvarado, the Balboa Canal visible in the background, international construction crews and AC officials on site. The artificial lake reflecting the partially completed building. Architectural documentary photograph style, clear tropical sky.]]

{{main|International City of Areza}}

The [[Treaty of Balboa Harbour]] (1960), which returned the Sant Cristòfor mandate to Balboa, did not return the canal zone. The canal zone remained under AC mandate, as the Adonian Community determined that the canal's continued international administration was necessary for the completion of the canal's operational upgrades and for the establishment of a permanent oversight mechanism.

The Treaty of Balboa Harbour established the [[International City of Areza]] as a permanently neutral international territory, carved from the canal zone on the north shore of [[Lake Alvarado]], the artificial lake formed by the canal's construction. Areza was intended to serve as the administrative seat of the AC's oversight of the Balboa Canal and the [[Twelve Guarantees]], and as the permanent home of the [[Adonian Community Assembly]]. The city was independent of Balboa: it maintained its own governance structures, its own military base (the [[AC Garrison Areza]]), its own tribunals, and its own legal system, based on AC mandate law. Areza's status was guaranteed in perpetuity.

The establishment of Areza within the canal zone mandate created a territorial anomaly: the mandate now contained an international city that was not subject to the High Commissioner's authority. Areza was administered directly by the Adonian Community's central institutions, and the High Commissioner's jurisdiction did not extend to the city's territory. The practical effect was that the canal zone mandate was divided into two zones: the Areza enclave, which was self-governing, and the remainder of the canal zone, which was administered by the High Commissioner.

== Termination ==

The canal zone mandate was returned to Balboa in 1973 under the [[Treaty of Areza]]. The treaty, negotiated by the third High Commissioner, [[Pere Rovira]], provided for the transfer of the canal zone to Balboan sovereignty, subject to the continuing provisions of the Twelve Guarantees and the permanent international oversight of the canal's operation.

The treaty established the following arrangements:

The canal itself was transferred to Balboan ownership and operation, with the proviso that the canal's tolls, tariffs, and operational regulations would be subject to review by the [[Adonian Community Canal Commission]], a body established by the treaty to ensure that the canal remained open to international traffic on non-discriminatory terms.

The town of Portnou and the civilian settlements along the canal corridor were transferred to Balboan civil administration, with the protections of the Twelve Guarantees applying to their populations.

The [[International City of Areza]] was excluded from the transfer and retained its status as a permanently neutral international territory, governed directly by the Adonian Community. The AC Assembly, which had been temporarily housed in Areza since 1960, was permanently seated in the city upon the completion of its purpose-built assembly building in 1973.

The [[International Cooperative Police Force]] (ICPF) retained its mandate in the canal zone and in Areza, operating under AC authority to ensure the enforcement of the Twelve Guarantees and the security of the canal and the international city.

The mandate was formally terminated on 1 July 1973, and the canal zone (excluding Areza) was transferred to Balboa. The ceremony was held in Sant Lázaro and was attended by the High Commissioner, the Balboan president, and representatives of the Adonian Community. Unlike the 1960 transfer of the Sant Cristòfor mandate, which had been met with silent protest, the 1973 transfer was received with cautious acceptance: the canal zone's civilian population, which had chafed under the mandate's canal-first priorities, was not unwilling to return to Balboan administration, provided that the Guarantees were enforced.

